WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.600
Right, let's dive straight into this explainer and

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.620
look under the bonnet of the abstract algorithms

3
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.700
that power our world.

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.420
I mean, we hear terms like algorithms and

5
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.700
data structures thrown around all the time, but

6
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.580
today we're actually going to demystify them.

7
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.680
We're exploring graph data structures, and a truly

8
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.600
brilliant technique called breadth-first search, which is

9
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.400
the magic behind everything from the social networks

10
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.360
we scroll through to the navigation apps that

11
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:22.880
guide us home.

12
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.320
Here is our roadmap for today.

13
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.140
First, what is a graph?

14
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.560
Two, understanding adjacency lists.

15
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.960
Three, queues in data structures.

16
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.820
Four, breadth-first search explained.

17
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.640
Five, tracing BFS step-by-step.

18
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.740
And finally, six, applying breadth-first search.

19
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.240
Chapter one, what is a graph?

20
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.460
The foundation of networks.

21
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.660
Before we can search a network, we absolutely

22
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.500
have to understand what it's actually made of.

23
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.960
So the crucial takeaway here is that a

24
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.340
graph is a non-linear data structure.

25
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.260
It isn't just a straight list.

26
00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.560
It's a web.

27
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.000
Think of it exactly like a physical map.

28
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.580
If you're looking at google maps, the intersections

29
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.880
of the roads are your vertices, or nodes,

30
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.660
and the actual roads connecting those intersections are

31
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:10.060
the edges.

32
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.360
It is a delightfully simple concept, but it

33
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.580
allows us to map out incredibly complex relationships.

34
00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.280
Now, you should know that edges aren't all

35
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.000
created equal.

36
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.340
Take an undirected graph, for example.

37
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:22.740
That's a bit like Facebook.

38
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.760
If you're friends with someone, they're friends with

39
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:24.920
you.

40
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.400
The relationship goes both ways.

41
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.520
But a directed graph, that is far more

42
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.520
like the world wide web.

43
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.460
A web page might have a link pointing

44
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.840
to another site, but that site doesn't necessarily

45
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:35.540
link back.

46
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.000
The travel is strictly one way, like a

47
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:38.600
one-way street.

48
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.380
Chapter two, understanding adjacency lists.

49
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.060
Mapping your neighbours.

50
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.600
Once we have a graph in our heads,

51
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.140
we need a way for a computer to

52
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.420
actually organise and process all those connections.

53
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.440
I mean, computers don't have eyes to look

54
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.540
at a drawing of a web, right?

55
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.700
So they use an adjacency list.

56
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.280
What's really fascinating here is how this list

57
00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.060
tells you exactly where you can travel in

58
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.260
just one single hop.

59
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.240
If you're standing on vertex zero, your immediate

60
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.340
neighbours, the places you can reach via a

61
00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.699
single edge, are vertices one and two.

62
00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.980
It's literally just a directory of neighbours.

63
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.700
Chapter three, queues in data structures.

64
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.160
Waiting in line.

65
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.060
To navigate our graph methodically, without getting completely

66
00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.580
and hopelessly lost, we need a specific data

67
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:23.260
management tool.

68
00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:24.740
Enter the queue.

69
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.120
A queue simply manages our data in a

70
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.220
strict, orderly fashion.

71
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.360
It's what we call a FIFO structure.

72
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:31.920
First in, first out.

73
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.560
Whoever arrives first is the absolute first to

74
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:34.880
be served.

75
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.800
Let's keep a really relatable mental model in

76
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:38.460
mind for this one.

77
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.620
It's exactly like waiting in a ticket line

78
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.100
at the cinema.

79
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.700
No cutting in line allowed.

80
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.760
As we're about to see, breadth-first search

81
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.420
uses this exact ticket line logic to decide

82
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.420
which vertex to visit next, ensuring total fairness

83
00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.200
in our exploration.

84
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.040
Chapter four, breadth-first search explained.

85
00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.160
Exploring level by level.

86
00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.460
Let's move to the main event and see

87
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.480
how this builds into the breadth-first search

88
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.160
algorithm, or BFS.

89
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.240
This is where the magic really happens.

90
00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.380
BFS operates in a strict four-step loop.

91
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.640
We add a starting point to our queue,

92
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.120
we take whoever is at the front, we

93
00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.920
mark them as officially visited so we don't

94
00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.060
process them twice, and then we tell all

95
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.800
their unvisited neighbours to go stand at the

96
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:20.300
back of the queue.

97
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.380
Because of this logic, BFS expands outwards, level

98
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:24.880
by level.

99
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.480
Just picture dropping a stone into a pond.

100
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.620
The exploration ripples outwards, visiting all the closest

101
00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.380
vertices before moving on to the ones further

102
00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:32.760
away.

103
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.600
Chapter five, tracing BFS step by step.

104
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.400
Tracking the queue and visits.

105
00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.520
The absolute best way to grasp this concept

106
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.400
is to trace it by hand, so follow

107
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.680
along and mentally queue these items up with

108
00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:45.840
me.

109
00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.080
We're going to start our search at vertex

110
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:48.480
zero.

111
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.980
In step one, we add our starting node,

112
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.140
vertex zero, to the back of our empty

113
00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:53.400
queue.

114
00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.380
And to ensure we don't walk in circles

115
00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.140
later, we immediately add zero to our visited

116
00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:58.480
set.

117
00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:00.420
Next up, step two.

118
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.880
Vertex zero is pulled from the front of

119
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:03.700
the queue to be processed.

120
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.740
The algorithm looks at zero's neighbours and sees

121
00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:07.560
one and two.

122
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.020
Since they haven't been visited yet, one and

123
00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.720
two grab tickets and join the back of

124
00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:12.000
the queue.

125
00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.580
We also mark them as visited right now

126
00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.040
so they aren't queued up again by another

127
00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:16.420
path.

128
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.399
Moving to step three, the next number in

129
00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:20.480
line is vertex one.

130
00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:21.700
It gets dequeued.

131
00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.300
Let's say its only unvisited neighbour is vertex

132
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:24.700
three.

133
00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.880
So vertex three is sent straight to the

134
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.840
back of the queue, right behind vertex two,

135
00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:30.100
who has been patiently waiting.

136
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:31.960
Notice what just happened.

137
00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.080
We finished exploring everything one hop away before

138
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.300
we even started queuing up things two hops

139
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:37.560
away.

140
00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.360
That is exactly the breadth in breadth-first

141
00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:40.720
search.

142
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.300
Finally, in step four, vertex two gets its

143
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.200
turn at the front of the line.

144
00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:46.440
It gets processed.

145
00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.700
But here is the really clever bit.

146
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.180
Its neighbours are zero and three.

147
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.060
But checking our visited set, the computer sees

148
00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.180
they are already marked.

149
00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.260
So it completely ignores them.

150
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.240
Actually, without this brilliant little check, the algorithm

151
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.300
would just bounce back and forth between vertices

152
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.440
in an infinite loop.

153
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.020
Chapter six, applying breadth-first search.

154
00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:08.480
Real-world uses.

155
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.280
So we've traced the logic, but why does

156
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.000
this actually matter for the apps we use

157
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:13.640
every single day?

158
00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.160
Well, because BFS always explores level by level,

159
00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.480
it guarantees that the very first time it

160
00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.660
discovers a destination, it has found the path

161
00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.200
with the absolute fewest edges.

162
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.580
It's a perfect shortest pathfinder.

163
00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.060
This is precisely the logic used in basic

164
00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.260
network routing, and it's the foundation of social

165
00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.320
media friend suggestions.

166
00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.180
When a platform suggests a mutual friend, it's

167
00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.140
essentially using graph theory to look at nodes

168
00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.780
that are exactly two hops away from you.

169
00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.080
But let me leave you with a bit

170
00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.100
of a provocative thought.

171
00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.480
BFS finds the path with the fewest edges.

172
00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.780
But what if we aren't just counting the

173
00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.840
number of roads, but the time it actually

174
00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:49.660
takes to travel them?

175
00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.080
What happens if a graph's edges have heavy

176
00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:52.700
traffic on them?

177
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.300
Well, BFS would still just count the edges,

178
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.400
meaning it might send you straight into a

179
00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.180
massive traffic jam.

180
00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.920
Solving that requires entirely new tools, which we'll

181
00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.840
be diving into in a future explainer.

182
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.320
Until then, the next time you get a

183
00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.700
friend suggestion, you'll know exactly how the computer

184
00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:09.660
queued up its search.
